Assuming `getUser` and `getOrders` return promises (or can be promisified), the refactored version is:

```javascript
const u = await getUser(id);
const o = await getOrders(u);
render(o);
```

This must run inside an `async` function:

```javascript
async function loadAndRender(id) {
  const u = await getUser(id);
  const o = await getOrders(u);
  render(o);
}
```

If `getUser` or `getOrders` only accept callbacks, wrap them first:

```javascript
const getUserAsync = (id) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) =>
    getUser(id, (u, err) => (err ? reject(err) : resolve(u)))
  );
```

Note that the original callback passes only `u` and no error argument. If your callbacks follow the Node.js `(err, result)` convention, the wrapper above handles errors. Otherwise, adjust the wrapper to match the actual signature.

