Here's an LRU cache in TypeScript using a `Map` (which preserves insertion order) for O(1) operations. Deleting and re-inserting a key moves it to the most-recently-used position, and the first key in the map is always the least recently used.

```typescript
class LRUCache<K, V> {
  private readonly capacity: number;
  private readonly cache: Map<K, V>;

  constructor(capacity: number) {
    if (capacity <= 0) {
      throw new RangeError("Capacity must be a positive integer");
    }
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map<K, V>();
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) {
      return undefined;
    }
    // Move key to most-recently-used position
    const value = this.cache.get(key) as V;
    this.cache.delete(key);
    this.cache.set(key, value);
    return value;
  }

  put(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      // Remove so it gets re-inserted at the end (MRU position)
      this.cache.delete(key);
    } else if (this.cache.size >= this.capacity) {
      // Evict least-recently-used (first entry in insertion order)
      const lruKey = this.cache.keys().next().value as K;
      this.cache.delete(lruKey);
    }
    this.cache.set(key, value);
  }
}

// Usage example
const cache = new LRUCache<string, number>(2);

cache.put("a", 1);
cache.put("b", 2);
console.log(cache.get("a")); // 1  (order: b, a)
cache.put("c", 3);           // evicts "b"
console.log(cache.get("b")); // undefined
console.log(cache.get("c")); // 3
```

**Complexity:** `get` and `put` are both O(1) amortized, since `Map` lookups, inserts, and deletes are hash-based, and fetching the first key via `keys().next()` is constant time.

If you need an explicit implementation (e.g., for an interview or a language without an ordered map), the classic approach pairs a `Map<K, Node>` with a doubly linked list. The `Map` version above is the idiomatic choice in TypeScript/JavaScript.

