A **race condition** happens when two or more threads (or processes) access shared data concurrently, at least one of them modifies it, and the final result depends on the unpredictable timing of how their operations interleave. Because `counter++` is really three steps (read, add, write), two threads can read the same old value and both write back the same increment, losing an update.

A **mutex** (mutual exclusion lock) fixes this by ensuring only one thread at a time can execute the critical section that touches the shared data.

**Example in C with pthreads:**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

#define ITERATIONS 1000000

long counter = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *increment(void *arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < ITERATIONS; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);    // enter critical section
        counter++;                    // shared data access
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // leave critical section
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Counter: %ld (expected %d)\n", counter, 2 * ITERATIONS);
    return 0;
}
```

Without the lock and unlock calls, the output would typically be less than 2,000,000 and vary from run to run. With the mutex, the output is always exactly 2,000,000.

**Key points:**
- Lock before touching shared state, unlock immediately after.
- Every thread that accesses the same data must use the same mutex.
- Keep critical sections short to limit contention.
